Time-to-resolve was not derivable, and MTTR was a structural zero
==================================================================
Date : 2026-07-29
Mode : unit + live on-prem chain (in-process webhook -> detector -> decision ->
       executor -> incident store) + dashboard tsc/next build
Gate : make check 1496 -> 1520 (+24)


What was wrong
----------------
Three defects in one chain, all of them green in the suite.

1. WRITERS DEFAULTED THE RESOLUTION TIME
   `aws/executor._record_incident` (the shared cloud writer for AWS/GCP/Azure)
   set `resolved_at` unconditionally to `recorded_at` — the same value it wrote
   into `created_at` on the same row. So an unresolved incident carried a
   resolution timestamp, and every resolved one reported a duration of zero.
   The on-prem writer had the mirror-image bug: it never wrote the field at all.

2. THE FETCH READ ONE KEY INTO BOTH ENDS
   `reporting._fetch_incidents_from_dynamo`:

       "started_at":   item.get("resolved_at", ""),
       "resolved_at":  item.get("resolved_at", ""),

   Every incident handed to the on-call reporter therefore began and ended at
   the same instant. `average_mttr_minutes` has been 0.0 in every weekly
   on-call report ever sent, and `mttr_delta_minutes` 0 on top of it.

   The same function also copied `alarm_name` into `runbook_id`, which collapsed
   recurring-pattern grouping back to per-alarm and hid the one thing it exists
   to show: distinct alarms landing repeatedly on one runbook.

3. THE DASHBOARD SCAN DID NOT FETCH WHAT ITS READERS READ
   The incidents `ProjectionExpression` named neither `triggered_at`,
   `confidence`, `reconciliation` nor `trace_id`. A DynamoDB Scan returns only
   the projected attributes, so all four arrived undefined no matter how
   carefully `mapIncidentRecord` handled them. Consequences: the incident detail
   view has rendered "confidence n/a" for every AWS-live incident since it
   shipped, the reconciliation panel never appeared, and this morning's
   `triggered_at` writer fix (gate 1496) stopped one layer short of the
   `detected +Nm` badge built to display it.


Why the suite did not catch any of it
---------------------------------------
`TestOncallReporter::test_summarizes_incidents` asserts an MTTR of 45.0 — off a
hand-written fixture with two different timestamps. That is a shape the only
real producer could not emit. The test was green for as long as it existed and
would have stayed green forever.

This is the third instance of the same lesson in three days: assert the
consumer, and feed it what production actually feeds it. The new tests in
tests/test_incident_time_to_resolve.py run `_fetch_incidents_from_dynamo`, not
a fixture shaped like its output.


Measured: the number the weekly report ships
----------------------------------------------
Two rows exactly as the executor writes them, fed through the real fetch.
Ground truth from the fire times: 30 min and 60 min, average 45.0.

BEFORE
  what the live fetch hands the reporter
    started_at='2026-07-29T00:30:00Z'  resolved_at='2026-07-29T00:30:00Z'
    started_at='2026-07-29T02:00:00Z'  resolved_at='2026-07-29T02:00:00Z'
  average_mttr_minutes = 0.0

AFTER
  what the live fetch hands the reporter
    started_at='2026-07-29T00:00:00Z'  resolved_at='2026-07-29T00:30:00Z'
    started_at='2026-07-29T01:00:00Z'  resolved_at='2026-07-29T02:00:00Z'
  average_mttr_minutes = 45.0


LIVE: the real on-prem chain, three paths
-------------------------------------------
Alertmanager payload with `startsAt` 25 minutes in the past, posted to the real
in-process webhook. Only the operator's severity label differs between runs.

  webhook responses: {'critical/P1': 200, 'info/P3': 200, 'warning/P2': 200,
                      'reject': 200}

  severity=P1  mode=AUTO  resolved=True
    triggered_at = 2026-07-28T19:24:48.405903Z
    created_at   = 2026-07-28T19:49:50.713851+00:00
    resolved_at  = 2026-07-28T19:49:50.713851+00:00
    -> TIME TO RESOLVE = 1502s (25m)   [badge: 'resolved in 25m']

  severity=P3  mode=MANUAL  resolved=False
    resolved_at  = <ABSENT>
    -> no resolution time: incident is OPEN (badge renders nothing)

  severity=P2  mode=APPROVE -> rejected  resolved=False
    resolved_at  = <ABSENT>
    -> no resolution time: incident is OPEN (badge renders nothing)

  1 resolved / 2 open — LIVE RESULT: PASS

The P2 row is the interesting one: it is written only when the human decision
lands, so under the old writer it would have claimed to be resolved at the
moment someone clicked reject.


Falsification (each fix reverted individually, then restored)
---------------------------------------------------------------
  1. cloud writer: restore unconditional `resolved_at`     -> 1 failed, 23 passed
  2. on-prem writer: drop `resolved_at` again              -> 1 failed, 23 passed
  3. fetch: `started_at` back to `resolved_at`             -> 4 failed, 20 passed
  4. fetch: `runbook_id` back to `alarm_name`              -> 1 failed, 23 passed
  5. reporter: restore the max(0.0, ...) clamp             -> 1 failed, 23 passed
  6. reporter: drop the parse guard                        -> 4 failed, 20 passed
  7. dashboard: restore the 4-field-short projection       -> 2 failed, 22 passed
  restored                                                 -> 24 passed


Design notes
--------------
- Absent, not defaulted. An open incident has no `resolved_at` at all, the same
  rule tenancy and `triggered_at` follow. "Not resolved" is actionable; "resolved
  in zero minutes" is a false claim that also drags the average.

- `_minutes_between` returns None rather than clamping. It used to be
  `max(0.0, ...)`, which counted a resolution timestamped *before* its fire time
  as a perfect zero-minute recovery. Spoke/hub clock skew is normal here, and
  skew is not a fast recovery — the sample is dropped instead.

- It also no longer raises. Four signal adapters feed `triggered_at` straight
  from `startsAt` / `firedDateTime` / `started_at`, so format variance (naive vs
  aware, epoch ints, junk) is reachable live now that the value actually flows.
  A raise there used to take down the entire weekly report, not one incident.

- The projection guard is derived, not a keyword list. It parses which
  attributes `mapIncidentRecord` reads and requires the Scan to fetch every one
  of them, so it fails for the *next* field somebody adds too. A hand-maintained
  list would have been written to match the projection as it stood and would
  have stayed green through exactly this bug.

- The `resolved in Nm` badge is part of the change, not decoration. Storing
  a resolution time with nothing reading it would have recreated the defect
  class this work exists to close.


Not done
----------
- No real DynamoDB round trip. The projection fix is verified by parsing the
  expression and by the mapper-coverage invariant; no AWS Scan was executed, so
  the corrected projection has not yet been observed returning the four fields
  from a live table. Same gap as gate 1496 noted for the write side.
- GCP's Firestore and Azure's Cosmos writers (`gcp/executor.py`,
  `azure/executor.py`) still record neither `resolved_at` nor `triggered_at`.
  Those are separate native stores that nothing in the dashboard or the weekly
  report reads, so fixing them would be writing fields with no consumer — left
  deliberately.
